{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeEF9qoBwphBTkp6kpzBA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All evidence, scripts, and write-ups are in the tree; the driver commits and pushes with the prepared message. **Ladder item 3.1 is done and marked.**\n\n## What I measured\n\nThe item read *\"`jetson_clocks` — GPU 1386 → 1575 MHz, EMC 2750 → 4266, measured in-repo at +3.0-6.4 %.\"* Both halves are wrong about this workload.\n\n**No run in this repo had ever sampled a clock while a decode was in flight.** Sampling both rails every 2 s across three governed arms: **97.7 % of compute-window samples are already at 1386 MHz and 4266 MHz** — the pinned frequencies. The 315/2750 state everyone read as \"the run\" is the ~90 s checkpoint load, when the GPU is idle and the CPU is reading 100 GiB. Both rails self-ramp within ~2 s of the GPU going busy.\n\nSo pinning buys the ramp, not the ceiling: **+1.99 ± 0.15 % (8/8 legs) — below the 3.5 % spread, not counted as a win.** `tau` is 3.8438 in all six arms across both runs, to four decimals. MAXN's 1575 MHz measured **+1.68 ± 5.83 %** — nothing, the right answer for a bandwidth-bound engine asked about its core clock.\n\n## Two findings larger than the item\n\n**Item 2.5 is answered.** `WARM decode` is a 7-step average taken immediately after load — *inside* the ramp — so it times the governor: **88.0/88.0/88.7/88.4 ms/tok governed vs 72.7/72.9 pinned**, six loads, reproducing to a tenth of a millisecond in both states. That 21 % artefact is the whole of 2.5's \"unexplained 17.4 % base-AR regression\". There is no engine regression. I left 2.5 unchecked — the mechanism is settled, but the consequence it names (every \"N.NNx vs base AR\" ratio in the repo has a denominator set by an unrecorded clock state) is an archive sweep deserving its own iteration.\n\n**The first attempt voided itself, and that is the more useful half.** `clocks_ab_run.sh` pre-registered \"if A′ does not come back to A the comparison is void\". A′ came back **+8.19 %** — larger than the effect. The cause was the supervising agent: it kept working during the arms, and a ~GB process of its own exited inside arm C's one backwards leg (22.97 → 14.37 tok/s with the clock flat at 1575 and power dropping 34 W → 19.5 W). Re-run on a quiet box, the drift control falls to +1.78 % and the engine reproduces `tau` on all 9 prompts instead of 8. **A measurement arm is single-tenant, and the agent is a tenant** — `tau`, LOSSLESS and the first-token gate all passed in all four contaminated arms; only repeating an already-run arm caught it.\n\n## Adopted — for the measurement, not the 2 %\n\n`scripts/pin_clocks.sh` (pin/restore/show/pin-maxn), called by `run_model.sh` and `run_server.sh` unless `DSV4_PIN_CLOCKS=0`, plus a `<log>.clocks` sidecar per run. HARDWARE.md has demanded since 2026-08-07 that every measurement state its clock state; nothing enforced it, so nothing did it. Exercised end-to-end, not just syntax-checked. The box is left governed at 120 W exactly as found, nothing is running, `detach_audit.sh` green, and `clocks_emc_probe.sh` is registered in its `PATTERNS`.\n\nWiki in the same commit: `negative-results.md` §4e, `measurement-and-traps.md` §23 + §24, `hardware-sm110a.md` §5, `HARDWARE.md`, and the README state table."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":776,"cache_read_input_tokens":151712,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":776},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd851466-8fe9-421b-9d36-f2763d695418","uuid":"0b16f274-efcc-4591-8dc7-b9b9450a541d","timestamp":"2026-08-20T15:34:34.760Z","request_id":"req_011CeEF9ohByKaEXKQFTf2Lz"}
